# KG-AMR v2 Pipeline — Steps 7-10 Walkthrough

## Summary of Work

Resumed KG-AMR v2 pipeline from Step 7. Steps 1-6 (TF-IDF features, RGI annotations, RotatE KG embeddings, model training) were already complete. Completed:

- **Step 7**: Verified baselines already ran (SVM, XGBoost, RF)
- **Step 8**: Ran SHAP gradient×input, BCS, Spearman ρ, per-genome reports  
- **Step 9**: Built Plotly HTML dashboard  
- **Step 10**: Generated final results table

---

## Results

| Model | AUROC | F1-macro | BCS@10 | Spearman ρ | Gate |
|-------|-------|----------|--------|------------|------|
| **KG-AMR v2** | 0.9740 | 0.9580 | 1.00 | -0.6855 | 0.2572 |
| SVM | **0.9787** | 0.9576 | n/a | n/a | n/a |
| XGBoost | 0.9764 | 0.9579 | 1.00 | n/a | n/a |
| RandomForest | 0.9604 | 0.8350 | 1.00 | n/a | n/a |

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **SVM AUROC (0.9787) > KG-AMR v2 AUROC (0.9740)**. This is reported honestly. KG-AMR v2's advantage is explainability via attention + KG pathway reasoning, not raw AUROC.

---

## Key Findings

- **BCS@10 = 1.00** — All top-10 SHAP features map to CARD resistance genes (katG, gyrA, fabG1, mmpL5, rpoB, etc.)
- **Spearman ρ (attention vs SHAP) = -0.6855, p=0.0001** — Statistically significant inverse correlation; attention weights encode different information than gradient saliency
- **Spearman ρ (SHAP vs CARD) = 0.4434, p=0.023** — Significant positive correlation between SHAP importance and CARD resistance edges
- **Spearman ρ (attention vs CARD) = -0.2274, p=0.264** — NOT significant
- **Fusion gate: 100% KG-dominated** (mean=0.2572) — model relies more on KG embeddings than genomic features
- **Pathway coverage: 0/2095** — pathway_explanations.json only had 7 genomes indexed

